        "title": "fix: severe per-message slowdown when commands.ownerAllowFrom has thousands of entries",
        "body": "Closes #50289\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nGateways with large `commands.ownerAllowFrom` lists can spend 15–27 seconds processing each message and emit `lane wait exceeded` warnings. The reporter has 9,282 entries. Command authorization repeatedly trimmed, parsed, channel-filtered, and plugin-formatted the complete list, then used linear array membership checks. That work ran for every inbound message and scaled with the configured list size.\n\nThe same behavior is present in the current release and on `main`. PR #103523 improves membership lookup but still constructs the prepared data on every message, so it does not remove the dominant repeated list transformation.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis change prepares each configured allow-from array once and carries those facts through the existing command-authorization owner boundary:\n\n- parsed entries and channel filtering;\n- plugin-formatted owner and command lists;\n- wildcard state; and\n- membership `Set`s.\n\nThe cache is deliberately scoped by the raw array, `OpenClawConfig` identity, actual plugin implementation identity, provider, account, and owner/command formatting mode. A config reload or plugin-registry replacement therefore cannot reuse stale formatted values. Per-message `ctx.OwnerAllowFrom` overrides retain their existing precedence.\n\nThis is the best-fit fix because it removes repeated request-time discovery at the authorization boundary instead of adding another per-message cache or only replacing `.includes`. The shipped mutable `CommandAuthorization.ownerList` SDK shape is preserved with a defensive copy at the public boundary, so callers cannot mutate cached authorization state. That compatibility copy and sender-candidate normalization still run per call; the claim is therefore that the expensive configured-list transformation is reused, not that every operation is strictly O(1).\n\n## User Impact\n\nWith a 10,000-entry `ownerAllowFrom`, the production-entry-point harness improved from **17.4 ms/message to 0.009 ms/message** (about 1,900×) while preserving owner, channel-prefix, wildcard, override, and command-allowlist behavior. Small configurations retain the same authorization semantics.\n\nThe change is internal to command authorization. It adds no config, migration, protocol, or Plugin SDK surface.\n\n## Evidence\n\nThe real-behavior harness calls `resolveCommandAuthorization` with 10,000 mixed channel-prefixed, numeric, and whitespace-padded entries, plus a Discord-shaped plugin formatter.\n\n**Before (`main`):**\n\n```text\n[PASS] listed sender is owner\n[PASS] unlisted sender is not owner\n[PASS] telegram-prefixed entry does not authorize a discord sender\ntiming: 200 messages, 10000 entries -> 3482 ms total, 17.408 ms/message\n[FAIL] authorization stays under 1 ms/message\n```\n\n**After (this branch):**\n\n```text\n[PASS] listed sender is owner\n[PASS] unlisted sender is not owner\n[PASS] telegram-prefixed entry does not authorize a discord sender\ntiming: 200 messages, 10000 entries -> 2 ms total, 0.009 ms/message\n[PASS] authorization stays under 1 ms/message\n```\n\n`src/auto-reply/command-auth.prepared.test.ts` adds 14 regression tests covering:\n\n- channel-prefixed, numeric, whitespace-padded, cross-channel, and unlisted entries;\n- compile-once behavior with a 5,000-entry list;\n- provider and account-sensitive formatting;\n- shared-array isolation across config identities;\n- shared config/array isolation across plugin-registry replacements with the same plugin ID;\n- mutation isolation for the SDK-facing `ownerList`;\n- `ctx.OwnerAllowFrom` precedence; and\n- provider-specific and global `commands.allowFrom` behavior.\n\nExisting focused authorization, Plugin SDK, command-control, stop-target, and subagent-routing suites remain in the CI selection. Repository formatting and `git diff --check` pass. Two independent Codex autoreviews (hardening diff and full PR branch) reported no actionable findings.\n\n[Secretless fork-PR CI run 29120968934](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29120968934) on exact head `9bb38e94dfd` passed production types, test types, every Node test shard, build artifacts, both QA smoke profiles, channel/plugin contracts, dependency guards, and security-fast. Its only failures are current-`main` baselines outside this diff: lint errors in `queue.drain-restart.test.ts` and `server.chat.gateway-server-chat.test.ts`, plus an ACP/agents Madge cycle. The immediately preceding [`main` run 29119824112](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29119824112) failed the same two jobs.\n\nKnown proof gap: the contributor-safe AWS Crabbox broker rejected this non-member account before allocating a lease, so no credential-hydrated Testbox was used. The pushed head was instead validated by the repository's secretless fork-PR CI above. A live 9k-user channel deployment was not available; the benchmark uses the production authorization entry point with the bundled Discord formatter's normalization shape. Config-file parsing reported in #50289 is a separate load-time surface and remains out of scope.\n\n---\n\nThis PR is AI-assisted (Claude Opus and Codex, human-operated). The final branch was independently reviewed after the cache-lifecycle and SDK-mutation regressions were added.\n",
        "branch": "perf/50289-command-auth-prepared-allowfrom",
